{:schema "kotoba-search-migration/v1"
 :profile :search/bounded-search-machinery-v1
 :date "2026-09-08"
 :supersedes-exclusion
 {:record "migration/bounded-index-and-weights-v1.edn"
  :claim "tokenize/score-doc/search/term-count need a per-code-point
          classification string primitive kotoba-lang/compiler does not yet
          expose -- its string ABI is UTF-8 byte-offset based, with no
          code-point-at / regex op."
  :verdict :stale-implementation-state
  :measurement
  "string-code-point-at IS an admitted language builtin (kotoba-lang/
   kotoba-lang lang/pure-product-profile.edn :string-ops) and
   kotoba.lang.bounded-text has walked code points with it since 2026-09-04.
   The claim was true of the amu the record was written against and false of
   amu main, and nothing re-measured it. Both halves measured 2026-09-08:
     amu f6ee539e (the pin deps.edn carried):
       kbb -M:kotoba compile src/search/bounded_search.kotoba \\
         --target js-browser --output /tmp/x.mjs --fuel 8192
       -> :kotoba/source-rejected \"operation has no admitted lowering\"
          at the (string-code-point-at text i) in step-boundary
     amu 9092ee34 (origin/main):
       same command -> :ok true, and the artifact RUNS: main() = 52
   deps.edn's :kotoba pin was moved to 9092ee34 with that reason beside it."}
 :legacy {:source "src/search/model.cljk" :status :retained-general-oracle}
 :canonical {:kernel-and-conformance "src/search/bounded_search.kotoba"
             :parity "test/search/bounded_search_kotoba_parity_test.cljk"
             :dual-backend-conformance "scripts/verify-bounded-search.mjs"}
 :domain
 {:functions [:token-count :token-start :token-end :token-text
              :term-count :term-count-checked :in-fold-subset?
              :field-weight-or :score-doc]
  :scope "search.model's SEARCH MACHINERY: the tokenizer (character class +
          CJK overlapping bigrams + case folding), term-count, and score-doc
          over the closed field-weight table."
  :ported-exactly [:search.model/term-count :search.model/score-doc]}
 :divergence
 {:no-collection-return
  {:what "search.model/tokenize returns a VECTOR of tokens; the guest exposes
          the INDEXED face instead -- token-count, plus token-start /
          token-end / token-text for the nth token."
   :why "This backend admits no readable collection of strings.
         kotoba.lang.bounded-text measured the same wall for join-text
         (2026-09-04) and left it deferred; string-split has the same shape,
         which is why segment-text / segment-count-text exist. Everything
         search.model builds ON tokenize is a count or a number, so
         term-count and score-doc port with no divergence at all."
   :status :measured-still-blocked
   :revisit "When a readable collection of strings lands, `tokens` is a
             version bump of this record, not a widening of the module."}
  :ascii-only-case-fold
  {:what "The .cljc folds with clojure.string/lower-case (locale case
          mapping); the kernel folds ASCII A-Z only."
   :measurement
   "Enumerated every Unicode code point on BOTH hosts (JVM ja_JP via
    clojure.string/lower-case, node via String.toLowerCase) asking which
    non-ASCII code points lower-case INTO [a-z0-9]. Both answered with the
    same TWO:
      U+0130 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER I WITH DOT ABOVE -> \"i\" + U+0307
      U+212A KELVIN SIGN                           -> \"k\"
    Those two, and only those two, are outside the kernel's subset."
   :handling "score-doc and term-count-checked REFUSE them through the error
              arm of [:result :i64 :string]; in-fold-subset? is the predicate
              on its own. A score of 0 is a real answer (\"no term matched\")
              and must never be read as \"not measured\", which is why the
              refusal is a type and not a sentinel."
   :test "refuses-exactly-where-the-fold-diverges pins BOTH halves: the guest
          takes the error arm, AND the oracle really does answer differently
          there. A refusal nobody can show a divergence for is decoration."}}
 :compiler-findings
 {:max-parameters
  {:measured "6 parameters is :kotoba.error/max-parameters (\"function
              parameters exceed ABI-supported arity\"); 5 compiles."
   :consequence "One walk answers both token edges with a `want-end` selector
                 rather than two walks, and score-doc factors the weight sum
                 per field instead of carrying q+title+tags+body+i+acc."}
  :declared-fuel
  {:measured "The reference JS runtime and the wasm32 module share a
              module-scoped fuel global whose DEFAULT is 512 calls. This
              kernel does not fit: at --fuel 7000 the compiler itself refuses
              with :kotoba/lowering-failed \"fuel-exhausted\" (it evaluates
              main), at 7500 it compiles and runs. The declared budget is
              8192."
   :consequence "term-count was rewritten as a SINGLE pass. Asking the
                 indexed face once per token to count matches is quadratic
                 and cost >20000 charges; the single pass fits in 8192 with
                 the whole self-check."
   :note "The parity runner re-imports the artifact per case because the fuel
          global is module-scoped; one instance would spend the budget on the
          first few cases."}
  :three-byte-cjk-invariant
  {:measured "Every code point in the tokenizer's CJK class lies in
              [U+0800, U+FFFF], so it is exactly three UTF-8 bytes."
   :consequence "A bigram is sliced arithmetically (s+3k .. s+3k+6) instead of
                 re-walking. main pins the invariant."}}
 :verification
 {:self-check "main returns FAILURES * 1000 + CHECKS-RUN -- never a boolean,
               which cannot tell one regression apart from a build that ran
               nothing. All-pass is 52."
  :web {:main 52 :required-capabilities []}
  :wasm {:main 52}
  :parity "202+ assertions against search.model over a 28-text corpus, 9 term
           cases, 5 score cases and 3 refusal cases. token-start/token-end are
           checked by RE-SLICING the original UTF-8 bytes on the JVM, so a
           guest returning plausible offsets cannot pass on token-text alone."
  :discrimination
  "Every check family was broken on purpose and seen to go red for the reason
   it names, then restored (2026-09-08):
     CJK class bound 40960 -> 40000      -> FIRST RUN STAYED GREEN. The corpus
       had no code point in [U+9C40, U+9FFF], so the upper class edge was
       untested. Boundary inputs were added at all four class edges (on the
       line and one code point outside); the same break then failed naming
       text 23 \"\\u9fff\\u9fff\" and the self-check reported 1 failure.
     bigram count (- cps 1) -> cps       -> token-face + self-check red
     bigram end +6 -> +3                 -> 20x \"token-start/token-end do not
                                            bound that token in the original
                                            bytes\" (the re-slice discriminates
                                            independently of token-text)
     fold the TERM as well as the text   -> term-count red on \"Slides\"
     :tags weight 3 -> 2                 -> score red, self-check 2 failures
     in-fold-subset? always true         -> \"guest answered instead of refusing\"
     one self-check expectation wrong    -> \"kernel self-check reported 1 failure(s)\"
     refusal case retargeted so the
       oracle does NOT diverge           -> \"the oracle does not actually
                                            diverge here, so this refusal
                                            guards nothing\"
   scripts/verify-bounded-search.mjs was shown red in both arms: 1 failing
   check, and a kernel that ran 50 checks instead of 52."}
 :cutover {:bounded-profile-authorized true :general-cljc-oracle-retained true}}
